    media: [
      { type: 'diagram', key: 'verification-filter-stack', title: 'The reality check, by era', caption: 'Oral memory bends; print holds a fixed page thousands can check; likes-and-shares count distribution, not truth; the settled Word outlasts every era - the one record that cannot be socially revised.' },
    ],
    levels: {
      teen: 'Every new way we share information changes what people call "true" — but Yahweh\'s Word was settled before any of it. "For ever, O LORD, thy word is settled in heaven." (Psalms 119:89). Jesus said, "Heaven and earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away." (Matthew 24:35). Now watch the Word answer five ages of "truth." (1) SPOKEN-ONLY DAYS. Before writing, truth bent to the tribe. Real example: an African kingdom\'s legend said the founder had SEVEN sons — then two provinces merged, and 60 years later everyone "remembered" FIVE. No record to catch it; scholars call it social amnesia. But a king once tried that on Yahweh\'s written scroll — he cut it and burned it (Jeremiah 36) — and Yahweh said, "Take thee again another roll, and write in it all the former words that were in the first roll" (Jeremiah 36:28). His record doesn\'t get erased. (2) WRITING FIXED TRUTH. Writing let truth stand outside one person\'s memory. Yahweh did it first — at Sinai the law came "written with the finger of God" (Exodus 31:18), and even the king had to hand-copy it so he couldn\'t quietly change it. (3) THE PRINTING PRESS. Identical printed copies let thousands check the same page and fix mistakes together — a 500-year age of "objective truth" the article calls the "Gutenberg Parenthesis." What it forgets: the first big thing printed was the Bible. (4) TV AND RADIO. Media started chasing what audiences WANT. The first "like" button was invented in 1937 to measure what listeners enjoyed — 70 years before social media. The Bible named this trap: people who "after their own lusts shall they heap to themselves teachers, having itching ears" (2 Timothy 4:3-4). (5) SOCIAL MEDIA. Now truth became a VOTE — likes and shares. As the article says, if print shared what mattered, digital treats as important "whatever is distributed." But Yahweh said, "Thou shalt not follow a multitude to do evil" (Exodus 23:2), and Jesus said the crowd\'s road is the wrong one: "wide is the gate, and broad is the way, that leadeth to destruction, and many there be which go in thereat" (Matthew 7:13). Popular isn\'t the same as true. (6) A.I. The article\'s scariest point: A.I. has no reality except us — it just keeps talking, and its words can lose their grip on real things ("A.I. slop"). But reality has an Author: "he is before all things, and by him all things consist" (Colossians 1:17). And the Word became a real Person: "the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us... full of grace and truth" (John 1:14). Our app builds the answer — every Bible verse is pulled word-for-word from a fixed copy and locked so it can\'t drift. (7) HOW TO SURVIVE IT. The article\'s advice: live a real life with real people, and READ DEEPLY. Those are old commands: don\'t stop "the assembling of ourselves together" (Hebrews 10:25), and "thou shalt meditate therein day and night" (Joshua 1:8), like the Bereans who "searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so" (Acts 17:11). The world\'s idea of truth may keep shifting; the Word settled in heaven never will.',
      senior: 'Truth in this house was settled before any medium existed: "For ever, O LORD, thy word is settled in heaven." (Psalms 119:89); "The grass withereth, the flower fadeth: but the word of our God shall stand for ever." (Isaiah 40:8); and Jesus staked His words above the cosmos: "Heaven and earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away." (Matthew 24:35). This week\'s essay (Andrey Mir, Big Think — its scholarship carried as the essay reports it, not re-verified at the underlying books) walks the ages of "truth"; the Word answers each. (1) ORALITY. Before writing, truth bent to the tribe — the state of Gonja "remembered" seven founding sons, then five after two chiefdoms merged, with no record to catch the seam (scholars call it social amnesia). But when a king cut and burned Yahweh\'s written scroll, Yahweh said, "Take thee again another roll, and write in it all the former words that were in the first roll" (Jeremiah 36:28) — His record is not erased. (2) WRITING. Writing separated truth from the teller; Yahweh did it first, at Sinai, in stone "written with the finger of God" (Exodus 31:18), with even the king commanded to hand-copy the law so he could not quietly change it. (3) PRINT. Identical copies let thousands correct the same page — the roughly 500-year "Gutenberg Parenthesis" of objective truth — which the essay never notes opened on the printed Bible. (4) ELECTRONIC MEDIA. Content chased the audience; the first "like" button was built in 1937 to measure what listeners preferred, seventy years before social media — the itching-ears economy named long before: people who "after their own lusts shall they heap to themselves teachers, having itching ears" (2 Timothy 4:3-4). (5) DIGITAL. Truth became a referendum — "whatever is distributed" is treated as significant. But "Thou shalt not follow a multitude to do evil" (Exodus 23:2), and the crowd\'s road is wide: "wide is the gate, and broad is the way, that leadeth to destruction, and many there be which go in thereat" (Matthew 7:13). Popularity is not truth. (6) A.I. The essay\'s fear: models have no reality but us, and their language can lose its grip on real things ("slop"). But reality has an Author — "he is before all things, and by him all things consist" (Colossians 1:17) — and the Word became flesh: "the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us... full of grace and truth" (John 1:14). This platform builds the answer: every verse is fetched verbatim from a fixed corpus and pinned so it cannot drift. (7) THE STRATEGIES ARE OLD COMMANDS. The essay\'s survival advice — embodied life and deep reading — the Word gave as covenant: "Not forsaking the assembling of ourselves together" (Hebrews 10:25); "thou shalt meditate therein day and night" (Joshua 1:8); the Bereans "searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so" (Acts 17:11). The world\'s notion of truth keeps shifting; the Word settled in heaven does not.',
    },
