      "research": "Find out, for one A.I. tool your family or the church actually uses this week, whether its weights are open or closed - that is, whether a copy could live on a machine we own, or whether every answer must cross somebody else's boundary. Write down which it is, and write down one capability claim made about it that you have personally never checked.",
      "plan": "Decide, before you touch anything, what your standard will be and where that standard lives. The rule here is that the alterable thing is never the standard: \"For ever, O LORD, thy word is settled in heaven\" (Psalms 119:89). So write the test down - what you will compare the tool's answer against, which text on disk, and who checks it. Then write the bound: what this tool may touch and what it may never touch. Both in plain words, before a line runs.",
      "execute": "Run the test once this week on a real case, with the written standard beside you, and record three things: what the tool answered, what the standard said, and whether they agreed. Then say out loud which of your claims about it are now proved and which are still only reported. \"Prove all things; hold fast that which is good\" (1 Thessalonians 5:21) - both halves, and the holding fast is the point of the proving."
